  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 309 AM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nLatest water vapor imagery details an upper-level trough \nlocated over the southern Canadian Prairies. This trough is \nexpected to continue moving gradually to the east-southeast this\nmorning. Closer to the surface on the eastern periphery of this\ntroughing pattern, a 1001 mb surface low rides east along the \nMN/Canadian border. A cold front extends SW from this low down \nthrough northwestern MN down through Madison, MN with a few \nisolated bands of rain showers across southern and western MN \nmoving northeast. Additionally a head of this cold front \nfeature, warm advective winds continue to keep temperatures in \nthe upper 50s to low 60s for numerous ob sites. But with the \ncold front expected to move from west to east today, these \nobserved warm temperatures now will likely be our forecast \nhighs for the day. Mid-level forcing is expected to increase as \nthe parent troughing pattern nears our vicinity today. Therefore\nhave maintained ~30-40% mentions for rain showers around \nsunrise this morning mostly across western MN and then \nincreasing in coverage across eastern MN into western WI by mid-\nmorning. Latest QPF trends favor western WI having the better \nchance of seeing between 0.50- 0.75\" of rain. While areas closer\nto I-35 will be closer between 0.10-0.25\" of rain. \n\nCanadian high pressure takes hold over the northern plains Monday \nand Tuesday which results in a colder airmass but its actually where \nwe are supposed to be in terms normal values. The peak of the colder \nair looks to occur Monday as temps fall into the teens. Once again, \nthe cold wont stick around for too long as \"warmer\" than normal \ntemperatures return for much of the northern CONUS by midweek near \n40 degrees. On Wednesday, another weak wave approaches from the west \nin the early morning hours which could perhaps produce a few flakes \nacross western Minnesota. However dry low levels could hinder flakes \nfrom reaching the ground. Have kept 20% mentions for light snow \nshowers as of now but likely will end up staying dry. By Friday into \nthe weekend, a stronger wave moves in from the NW. This one does \nlook to have a better organized surface low although location and \ntrack diverge quickly. Latest NBM has advertised 15-20% chance \nfor a rain/snow mix but lots to watch for first before any \nconfidence can be said on snow impacts (if any) for that event.",
